{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-129772",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Previously, we found that the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{3}(x)=2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nand the Maclaurin series for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=1-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{4}-⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find the expansion of $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we multiply the expansions of $f(x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ as follows: \n\n$$\nf(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=(2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3})(1-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{4}-⋯)\n$$\n\nLet's work out each term individually:\n\n* There is a constant term, given by\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\n* There is a single $x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}x\\cdot 1=-\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\n* There are two   ${x}^{2}$  terms and their sum is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (-2{x}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot 1 & =-4{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}, \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll other terms contain powers of $x$ greater than $2.$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ about $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{15}{4}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
